  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 300 AM Tuesday... \n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... There is increased forecast confidence that coastal \nlow pressure off the South Atlantic coast this weekend will spread \nrain across the ern half of NC by Sat night-Sun.\n\nThe models indicate a shortwave perturbation will develop, within an \nincreasingly-energetic srn stream jet forecast to strengthen from \nnear and east of HI to the GOM, and pivot across the South Atlantic \nstates this weekend. As it does so, it will likely interact with and \nenergize an east/west-oriented low-level baroclinic zone and \ncontribute to cyclogenesis invof South FL and The Bahamas. The trend \nin the 00Z deterministic GFS/ECMWF/CMC has been toward a deeper \ncyclone and with a more nwd to nnewd track along and just offshore \nthe South Atlantic coast through the weekend. These solutions have \nmore support from the 00Z AIFS and AIGEFS than they do from their \nrespective global dynamical ensembles. While convective \namplification (via latent heat release) of the cyclone will be \npossible, it should be noted that each of the aforementioned 00Z \ndeterministic solutions depict (varying degrees of) gridscale \nfeedback and development/deepening of the cyclone as it tracks near \nthe South Atlantic coast. The locations of the related QPF maxima \nand influences to deepening of the cyclone will undoubtedly shift in \nsubsequent forecast cycles, so additional fluctuations in low track \nand intensity may likewise result. Given both the prevalence of that \nfeedback in model solutions and also the relative lack of support \nfrom the global dynamical ensembles at this time, forecast \nconfidence is still only low to medium, despite the overall \nincreasing signal for cyclogenesis near the South Atlantic coast. \nThe potential for rain, possibly heavy, has nonetheless increased \nfor particularly the ern half of NC during the latter half of the \nweekend (most likely Sat night-Sun).",
